  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nGeneral CONUS riding will persist through the work week before \ntroughing off the southern California coast begins to move \nashore by Thursday morning. Significant differences begin to \nemerge among cluster regarding to the speed of the trough \nejection into the plains with some as early Friday and some \nslower solutions bringing the main trough into the Dakotas on \nSaturday. Either way rain chances increasing this weekend but \nlikley not a soaker as probs for greater than a quarter inch \npeak at about 50% with other clusters as low as 30%. Other \nnotable features through the period will be strong winds Friday \nand Saturday though RH does not look to be particularly \nconcerning so would be simple wind headlines if warranted. A \nweak shortwave moving over the top of the ridge through Canada \nwill again bring a weak frontal boundary/wind shift through on \nWednesday, but not much impact expected. Temperatures will \nremain above seasonal averages throughout the period with the \nECMWF EFI showing only more of a warm signal as we head into \nnext weekend and flow turns southwesterly.\n\n- Rain chances/Wind\n\nRain could come in multiple waves depending how nebulous or \nwell organized the shortwave activity is that emanates from the \nsouthwest flow late this week. Overall the most robust QPF \nlooks to stay to our north and west per clusters but still \nseeing hints of an EML advecting north with MUCAPE in the \nhundreds bringing thunder chances. This would make amounts much \nmore variable but still would expect amounts to remain under an \ninch even if thunderstorms do manifest given the ensemble 95th \npercentile accumulation peaking below 1\" through Sunday. With \nthe numerous waves will also come enhanced surface flow with all\n3 ensemble systems (GEFS, EC, Canadian) also showing a strong \ncold front later in the day Friday. NBM QMD max wind gusts shows\ngusts Friday/Saturday in the 40-50 mph range. EFI also has some\nshading in the 0.5-0.7 range which indicates above normal climo\nvalues but maybe not approaching headlines. We will see how \nthis plays out. \n\nOverall enjoy a few days of 60s to 70s for highs this week as \nthey will become increasingly fleeting in the coming weeks with \nseasonal norms dropping into the 40s by the end of the month for\nhighs. Finally, after a widespread morning of temps in the \nupper 20s last night we are officially ending frost and freeze \nheadlines for the season.",
